-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----


Hello,

There is a new NIS+ project for Linux. The goal is to implement a fully
functional NIS+ support for Linux, which will work with Linux libc 5.x and
GNU libc 2.x. Maybe the rpc.nisd daemon will be implemented later, too.

There is a mailing list (nisplus@vt.uni-paderborn.de). You could subscribe
to it by sending an mail to Majordomo@vt.uni-paderborn.de with the line
"subscribe nisplus" in the body. More Information and the sources are on
http://www-vt.uni-paderborn.de/~kukuk/linux/nisplus.html

Current status:

 * NIS+ API:
   All nis_* functions are implemented. The FOLLOW_PATH flag is missing and
   there is no real callback in the moment. This functions are in the glibc
   snapshot for the upcoming glibc 2.1 (not in glibc 2.0.x!). There are
   patches for Linux libc 5.x.
   This implementation uses the /var/nis/NIS_COLD_START file and works
   with DES authentication (rpc.nisd with secure level 2).

 * getXXent/getXXbyYY functions:
   This functions are all implemented for glibc. For libc 5.x we uses the
   NYS functions, only the functions for passwd, group, hosts and publickey
   are implemented.

 * nistools (nisinit, niscat, nispasswd, ...):
   Some are implemented, some more are missing.

TODO:

 * Implement a NIS+ cache like SUN's nis_cachemgr and
   /var/nis/NIS_SHARED_DIRCACHE file
 * Implement the FOLLOW_PATH flag and use the callback feature from rpc.nisd.
 * Test all functions
 * Integreate the NIS+ functions in libc 5 (With a seperate libnsl)
   and implement the missing getXXent/getXXbyYY functions.

- -- 
Thorsten Kukuk  kukuk@vt.uni-paderborn.de
                http://www-vt.uni-paderborn.de/~kukuk/
Linux is like a Vorlon.  It is incredibly powerful, gives terse,
cryptic answers and has a lot of things going on in the background.


